; workload-vm: the host-global SELinux delta VM workloads need once QEMU is
; confined as svirt_t (ADR 006 step 3).
;
; The connected version of everything below — the share's ownership model, why
; the sidecar holds no capabilities, what confines it, and a symptom table — is
; docs/vm-virtiofs.md. This file carries the per-rule why.
;
; Two facts live here, and they have the same root: workloadctl runs QEMU as
; svirt_t *outside libvirt*, so two things libvirt does for QEMU we do
; differently, and the shipped policy is written around libvirt's way.
;
;   1. A domain for the virtiofsd sidecar (the bulk of this file), because
;      libvirt's virtiofsd inherits a domain we have no parent to inherit from.
;   2. `allow svirt_t passt_t:process signal`, because libvirt spawns passt
;      itself while QEMU's native -netdev passt makes QEMU passt's parent.
;
; A NOTE ON VOLUMES. wlvfsd_t is granted the types workloadctl itself labels:
; svirt_image_t for a workload's own tree, qemu_var_run_t for the socket dir. A
; volume pointing at an operator path elsewhere carries whatever label that path
; already has (/srv is var_t, for instance) and the sidecar will be denied it.
; That is not something this module can pre-empt without granting the union of
; every type on the host; `workloadctl diagnose` reporting the denial is the
; right answer, and the fix is an fcontext rule on the operator's path — or, on
; a filesystem that cannot hold a label at all (cifs, nfs: `fs_noxattr_type`,
; one genfscon type for every inode), a `context=` mount option, since fcontext
; has nothing to write to there. docs/vm-virtiofs.md §5 has both, and the
; misleading error the second case produces.
;
; WHY THE VIRTIOFSD DOMAIN EXISTS
;
; Once a VM workload's QEMU is confined as svirt_t (ADR 006 step 3), virtiofs
; volumes break, because SELinux checks `connectto` against the *peer process's
; domain*, not the socket file's label:
;
;   denied { connectto } path="/run/workload-vm/<name>/virtiofs-t0.sock"
;       scontext=svirt_t tcontext=unconfined_service_t tclass=unix_stream_socket
;
; Fedora 44 ships no virtiofsd domain at all — no virtiofsd_t, no
; virtiofsd_exec_t, no fcontext entry; /usr/libexec/virtiofsd is plain bin_t.
; (virtiofs_t exists, but that is the *filesystem* type.) So systemd execs our
; sidecar into unconfined_service_t, and svirt_t may not connect to it.
;
; This is not an upstream gap waiting to be filled. libvirt never labels
; virtiofsd: eight of its QEMU helpers get a child-process label through
; qemuSecurityCommandRun, and qemu_virtiofs.c is the one that does not — it
; launches with a plain virCommandRun, so the daemon inherits its parent's
; domain. Under system-mode libvirt that parent is virtqemud_t, which is in the
; virt_driver_domain attribute that svirt_t may already connect to; system
; libvirt works by inheritance rather than by design. A bare systemd sidecar has
; no such parent. libvirt has no virtiofsd domain because inheritance covers its
; cases, and the policy has none because libvirt never asked.
;
; WHY A DOMAIN RATHER THAN THE BLANKET ALLOW
;
; The cheap fix is one rule:
;
;   allow svirt_t unconfined_service_t:unix_stream_socket connectto;
;
; and it has distro precedent — selinux-policy-40.26-1.fc40 shipped exactly this
; shape for unconfined_t ("Allow svirt_t connect to unconfined_t over a unix
; domain socket"), still live in 44.5. But it lets any confined VM reach any
; unconfined service socket on the host, and it was only ever the cheaper option
; because the cost of the alternative was unmeasured. It was then measured: the
; domain below is ordinary rules, no user-namespace machinery, because
; generate_virtiofs_service passes --sandbox=none and confines the daemon by
; other means (see the next section). (Contrast ../../security/pasta_sandbox.cil,
; which exists precisely because pasta's userns+pivot_root sandbox needs
; cap_userns and tmpfs_t:dir mounton. Switching the sidecar to
; --sandbox=namespace would reopen that cost.)
;
; WHAT CONFINES THE SIDECAR
;
; generate_virtiofs_service runs it as _wl-<name> with an empty capability
; bounding set, which the id map is what permits: every guest id translates to
; the one host uid, so virtiofsd never switches credentials and never needs
; CAP_SETUID. Three things confine it, and virtiofsd's own --sandbox is not among
; them — chroot mode requires euid 0, so an unprivileged daemon runs
; --sandbox=none:
;
;   * DAC — it is the workload's own unprivileged user, so the share is the only
;     interesting thing it can write even with no policy at all.
;   * The unit's mount namespace — ProtectSystem=strict, ProtectHome=,
;     ProtectProc=invisible, and a ReadWritePaths= naming exactly the share and
;     the socket directory.
;   * This module, which is therefore the whole of the type enforcement rather
;     than a backstop behind a sandbox of virtiofsd's own.
;
; What that buys: a process that cannot chown, cannot setuid, and holds no
; capability to abuse in the first place. What it costs: the daemon no longer
; restricts its own view of the filesystem, so the mount namespace and this
; module have to be right.
;
; A dedicated type also separates virtiofsd from QEMU directly, so MCS
; categories stay unused (ADR 006 §9.5) — under the blanket allow, every VM's
; sidecar would share one type and only categories could tell them apart.
;
; WHY THIS IS HOST-GLOBAL AND NOT A PER-WORKLOAD BUNDLE
;
; Nothing here is per-workload: one process type, one exec type, one entrypoint,
; and one permission granted to svirt_t. Shipping it through the per-workload
; `[security].selinux_policy` bundle would mean N identical copies racing
; semodule, gated on a flag that has nothing to do with whether virtiofs works.
; The RPM installs and loads it once. The bundle keeps its existing job, the
; per-workload QEMU delta.
;
; NOTE FOR DEPLOYED HOSTS: the policy store lives in /etc, which ostree
; 3-way-merges, so a `bootc upgrade` does NOT deliver a changed module to a
; machine whose store has local modifications (the same caveat the image's
; Containerfile records for its own CIL modules). The store is in /etc because
; the image's semanage.conf sets `store-root=/etc/selinux`; Fedora's own default
; is /var/lib/selinux, which ostree would replace wholesale.
;
; This is the expected state, not a rare one: `semanage fcontext -a` rewrites
; the store, and every `workloadctl enable` runs one. Measured on a deployed
; host — 493 of ~639 diverged /etc paths were the policy store, this module's
; directory among them.
;
; `workloadctl diagnose` therefore reports both halves: whether the module is
; loaded, and whether the loaded copy still matches the .cil this build ships
; (semodule keeps the source verbatim under <store>/active/modules/<prio>/, so
; the comparison is exact). A module that is present but stale used to pass
; every check. The fix is `semodule -i /usr/share/workloadctl/workload-vm.cil`
; — /usr is replaced wholesale, so the shipped file is always current.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Declarations
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(type wlvfsd_t)
(typeattributeset domain (wlvfsd_t))
(roletype system_r wlvfsd_t)

(type wlvfsd_exec_t)
; Required before the filecon below can name it: a type used in a file context
; must be associated with object_r, or semodule rejects the module with
; "Type wlvfsd_exec_t is invalid for role object_r".
(roletype object_r wlvfsd_exec_t)
(typeattributeset file_type (wlvfsd_exec_t))
(typeattributeset exec_type (wlvfsd_exec_t))
(typeattributeset entry_type (wlvfsd_exec_t))

; Retyping the binary is what keeps the entrypoint narrow. virtiofsd is
; unlabelled (bin_t), so a domain that took bin_t as its entrypoint would make
; *every* bin_t binary an entry into wlvfsd_t. This is the same shape the
; shipped policy already uses for swtpm and passt — and, one directory over, for
; QEMU itself: /usr/libexec/qemu-kvm is qemu_exec_t, not bin_t.
;
; A filecon is safe here specifically. file_contexts.local outranks the base
; file_contexts wholesale, so most-specific-wins applies only within one source
; — which is why the per-workload svirt_image_t rule must go through semanage
; and cannot ship as a filecon. Nothing registers /usr/libexec in
; file_contexts.local, so this rule competes with the base /usr/libexec/.*
; entry within one source, where specificity does apply.
(filecon "/usr/libexec/virtiofsd" file (system_u object_r wlvfsd_exec_t ((s0) (s0))))

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Entry: a type transition on the retyped binary, no wrapper
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The sidecar needs none of the runcon machinery the QEMU unit uses, because it
; has a real exec to transition on rather than a Python wrapper.
(typetransition init_t wlvfsd_exec_t process wlvfsd_t)
(allow init_t wlvfsd_exec_t (file (getattr open read execute map)))
(allow init_t wlvfsd_t (process (transition)))
(allow wlvfsd_t wlvfsd_exec_t (file (entrypoint execute map getattr ioctl open read)))

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; What virtiofsd actually does
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; HOW TO REPRODUCE THIS LIST, AND WHY THE METHOD IS THE WHOLE OF IT.
;
; A permissive harvest yields exactly the classes the harvest exercised, and
; nothing warns about the rest. Under-covering it does not produce a module that
; fails; it produces one that serves a share which is healthy until a single
; ordinary operation is not — a `ln -s` that returns EPERM while every read,
; write, create and delete around it succeeds.
;
; So do not extend this list from a denial in isolation. Rebuild it: remove every
; wlvfsd_t rule, mark the domain permissive with dontaudit disabled (`semanage
; permissive -a wlvfsd_t; semodule -DB`), and drive a live guest across the whole
; filesystem surface — create/read/write/truncate/append, mkdir/rmdir, symlink
; read+create+rename+delete, hard link, FIFO and unix socket create and delete,
; chmod/chown/utimes on both files AND directories, rename within a directory and
; across directories, statfs, a first-boot cloud-init, and a clean stop. What
; that produces is what belongs here. If a new denial appears, extend the
; WORKOUT and rebuild, rather than appending one permission.
;
; The classes an incomplete workout drops are predictable, and they are the ones
; below `file`: lnk_file, fifo_file, sock_file, plus dir:setattr, dir:reparent
; and file:link. A workout of files and directories alone finds none of them.
;
; THERE IS NO CAPABILITY RULE, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
;
; The unit sets CapabilityBoundingSet= empty, so the process holds nothing
; SELinux could be asked to permit — a capability rule here would be text that
; can never be reached. If a capability denial appears for wlvfsd_t, the UNIT
; changed, and the question to ask is why it needs the privilege, not whether to
; add the allow.
;
; sys_resource in particular is unnecessary because the unit sets
; LimitNOFILE=1000000: systemd raises the limit while it still has the privilege,
; so virtiofsd finds its target already met and never asks for a hard-limit
; raise.

; The shared dir lives under /var/lib/workloads/<name>/, which a VM workload's
; per-workload fcontext rule labels svirt_image_t at enable. Serving FUSE means
; the full create/read/write/unlink surface, not just reading the directory
; (ADR 006 §9.7).
;
; `setattr` covers chown/chmod/utimes arriving from the guest, on directories as
; well as files. A share is a filesystem, not a blob store: the guest owns the
; metadata on it, and a permission model that lets a guest create a file but
; never chown it is not a subset of "share a directory" — it is a different,
; broken thing. The most visible casualty is cloud-init, which chowns the default
; user's home on first boot and is silently denied.
;
; The three classes below `file` are all the same story and it is worth naming
; once: a share is a filesystem, and a guest that can create a kind of node but
; not remove it, or move it, has a filesystem with a hole in it. lnk_file was the
; live bug — under enforcing, `ln -s` anywhere in the share failed with EPERM
; while every other operation succeeded, so the share looked healthy right up
; until something ordinary needed a symlink (a package install, a build tree, a
; git checkout). fifo_file and sock_file are the same shape one step further out:
; a build that opens a FIFO, or any daemon the guest runs that binds a unix
; socket inside its own home, would have hit it next.
;
; `rename` and `reparent` are on the dir line for the same reason: `mv` within a
; directory needs rename, and `mv` into a different one needs reparent as well.
; `link` on the file line is hard links.
(allow wlvfsd_t svirt_image_t (dir (add_name create getattr open read remove_name rename reparent rmdir search setattr write)))
(allow wlvfsd_t svirt_image_t (file (create getattr link open read rename setattr unlink write)))
(allow wlvfsd_t svirt_image_t (lnk_file (create getattr read rename unlink)))
(allow wlvfsd_t svirt_image_t (fifo_file (create getattr unlink)))
(allow wlvfsd_t svirt_image_t (sock_file (create getattr unlink)))

; statfs on the backing filesystem, which the guest reaches through `df` on the
; mount. Read-only and about the filesystem rather than any file on it; without
; it `df /mnt/share` in the guest fails with EPERM while every actual read and
; write succeeds, which reads as a broken mount rather than a missing rule.
;
; TWO TYPES, because the `filesystem` class checks the SUPERBLOCK's label, not a
; file's. An xattr filesystem's superblock is fs_t and the files on it carry
; their own labels, so the first rule covers every ordinary volume. A
; single-label filesystem (cifs, nfs — anything `fs_noxattr_type`) has no
; xattrs, so it can only be labelled by mounting it with `-o context=`, and that
; option sets the superblock's type as well as every file's. A share mounted
; `context=system_u:object_r:svirt_image_t:s0` therefore arrives with an
; svirt_image_t superblock, and the fs_t rule does not reach it.
;
; Measured on a live cifs share: read, write, mkdir and unlink all succeed while
; `df` alone is denied — `denied { getattr } dev="cifs" tclass=filesystem
; tcontext=...:svirt_image_t`. See docs/vm-virtiofs.md §5 for the operator half.
(allow wlvfsd_t fs_t (filesystem (getattr)))
(allow wlvfsd_t svirt_image_t (filesystem (getattr)))

; /var/lib/workloads itself keeps the blanket container_file_t rule; only the
; per-workload subdirectory is svirt_image_t. So reaching the share means
; traversing a container_file_t directory. Search only — this is a path
; component, not something the sidecar reads.
(allow wlvfsd_t container_file_t (dir (search)))

; The guest's memory. QEMU backs it with a memfd labelled svirt_tmpfs_t and
; virtiofsd maps it to move FUSE payloads without copying. This is the other
; half of what only appears once a guest does real I/O.
(allow wlvfsd_t svirt_tmpfs_t (file (map read write)))

; The vhost-user control socket QEMU hands over. Same shape as the passt rule
; below: a socket created by QEMU, so labelled svirt_t, that the helper must
; use. Also dontaudit'd upstream, so it does not appear in an ordinary harvest.
(allow wlvfsd_t svirt_t (unix_stream_socket (read write)))

; /run/workload-vm(/.*)? is svirt_var_run_t — an alias of qemu_var_run_t, which
; CIL resolves to. Registered by the RPM's %post, not per workload.
;
; `file` is the pid file virtiofsd writes and flocks next to the socket; the
; sock_file line is the socket itself. remove_name/unlink are the exit path, so
; they only appear in a harvest that reaches a clean stop. The socket needs no
; `setattr`: the unit passes no --socket-group, because the socket is already
; owned by the user QEMU runs as.
(allow wlvfsd_t qemu_var_run_t (dir (add_name getattr remove_name search write)))
(allow wlvfsd_t qemu_var_run_t (file (create getattr lock open write)))
(allow wlvfsd_t qemu_var_run_t (sock_file (create unlink)))

; The journal, via inherited stdout/stderr (StandardOutput=journal). There is
; deliberately no `connectto` and no NSS block — no systemd_userdbd_t,
; systemd_homed_t or passwd_file_t: resolving a user or group name is something
; only --socket-group=<user> would ask for, and the unit does not pass it.
(allow wlvfsd_t init_t (unix_stream_socket (ioctl read write)))

; /proc/sys/fs/nr_open, read once at startup by the rlimit setup.
(allow wlvfsd_t sysctl_fs_t (dir (search)))
(allow wlvfsd_t sysctl_fs_t (file (getattr open read)))

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; What svirt_t needs that the shipped policy does not give it
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The line the virtiofsd domain exists for: replaces the blanket
; `svirt_t -> unconfined_service_t` connectto with a grant scoped to exactly
; the daemon QEMU is supposed to be talking to.
(allow svirt_t wlvfsd_t (unix_stream_socket (connectto)))

; passt transitions to passt_t for free once QEMU is svirt_t (the shipped policy
; carries `type_transition svirt_t passt_exec_t:process passt_t`), but the
; shipped policy does not let svirt_t *signal* passt_t — because under libvirt
; it never needs to. libvirt spawns passt itself, as its own child. We use
; QEMU's native `-netdev passt`, so QEMU is passt's parent and signals it on
; shutdown. Without this, every VM stop leaks a passt process.
;
; This is the cost of choosing the native netdev over an externally-managed
; passt. Measured on a live VM: denied on every stop, and nowhere else.
(allow svirt_t passt_t (process (signal)))

; The other half of the same fact, and the one that actually stops the VM
; working. QEMU's native netdev creates a socketpair and forks passt with one
; end already open; libvirt instead starts passt separately and connects to it
; by path. So under our topology passt has to read and write a unix_stream_socket
; labelled svirt_t, which the shipped policy does not allow — it grants passt_t
; only `svirt_t:fd use` and the fifo_file pair.
;
; This one is worth dwelling on, because it is INVISIBLE BY DEFAULT: the shipped
; policy dontaudits it, so the denial produces no AVC at all. What is observed
; instead is passt failing with "Failed to add fd to epoll: Operation not
; permitted" and QEMU respawning it in a tight loop — 1563 suppressed denials in
; 30 seconds, an unreachable guest, and an audit log with nothing in it. It only
; became visible under `semodule -DB`. Anyone re-deriving this module's contents
; from a fresh audit harvest will miss it the same way; disable dontaudit first.
(allow passt_t svirt_t (unix_stream_socket (read write)))
